%S A400364 337,21361,54721,354481,1521361,2566561,4715281,12974641,14459041,
%T A400364 43273441,44724481,96219601,81534961,157402561,181520161,390925201,
%U A400364 355796401,748512241,1049890321,878044801,1446515281,2398403281,1993324321,2115389761,4229299201
%N A400364 Sum of the fourth powers of the legs of the primitive Pythagorean triangle with prime hypotenuse A002144(n).
%C A400364 If p = A002144(n) = x^2 + y^2 with integers x > y > 0, then the legs are u = x^2 - y^2 and v = 2*x*y, and a(n) = u^4 + v^4. Either leg may be the longer one. Terms are listed in increasing order of p, not in increasing order of a(n); for example, a(12) > a(13).
%C A400364 A400363 lists the hypotenuses p for which a(n) is prime.
%C A400364 For p = A002144(n), let Q = p*a(n). The construction in A400365 gives a 3 X 3 magic square with central entry Q^2 and nine distinct positive integer entries, at least six of them squares. This works whether a(n) is prime or composite.
%H A400364 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400364/b400364.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2549</a>
%H A400364 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquares.htm">Magic squares of squares</a>.
%H A400364 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02985794">Some notes on the magic squares of squares problem</a>, The Mathematical Intelligencer, 27 (2005), 52-64.
%H A400364 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquaresSearch.htm">Latest research on the "3x3 magic square of squares" problem</a>.
%H A400364 Lee Morgenstern, <a href="https://www.multimagie.com/English/Morgenstern14.htm">Euler's Formulas for 3x3 Magic Squares of 6 Squares</a>, December 2011.
%H A400364 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400365/a400365.txt">Fourth powers of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%F A400364 a(n) = A002365(n)^4 + A002366(n)^4.
%F A400364 If p = A002144(n) = x^2 + y^2 with integers x > y > 0, then a(n) = (x^2 - y^2)^4 + (2*x*y)^4 = p^4 - 8*x^2*y^2*(x^2 - y^2)^2.
%e A400364 The first Pythagorean prime is 5, whose primitive triangle has legs 3 and 4. Thus a(1) = 3^4 + 4^4 = 337.
%e A400364 The next two hypotenuses are 13 and 17, with legs (5,12) and (8,15), respectively. Consequently, a(2) = 5^4 + 12^4 = 21361 = 41*521 and a(3) = 8^4 + 15^4 = 54721.
%o A400364 (Python)
%o A400364 from math import isqrt
%o A400364 from sympy import primerange
%o A400364 def a400364(limit=50000):
%o A400364     # Return terms for hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A400364     out = []
%o A400364     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A400364         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A400364             continue
%o A400364         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A400364             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A400364             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A400364                 out.append((x*x - y*y)**4 + (2*x*y)**4)
%o A400364                 break
%o A400364     return out
%Y A400364 Cf. A002144, A002365, A002366, A002645, A400363, A400365.
%K A400364 nonn,easy,new
%O A400364 1,1
%A A400364 _Edward Schmidt_, Sep 24 2026
